  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1051 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nHigh clouds have spread across the area while lower clouds from\nearlier have cleared. Winds are out of the southeast. Looking at\nsatellite we have a couple of main features that will aid in the\nheavy rain forecast for the next couple of days. We see an\nupper-level trough over the Great Basin and Sierra Nevadas, and\nHurricane Polo along the southern coast of Baja California.\nHurricane Polo will inject additional tropical moisture into \nthe warm conveyor belt ahead of the upper-level trough leading \nto a significant amount of moisture advection into west Texas, \nNew Mexico, Colorado, Kansas, and Nebraska over the next couple\nof days. \n\nThe first round of rain arrives tomorrow morning as the western\ntrough pushes eastward, shifting the warm conveyor belt into\neastern Nebraska. Expect a complex of showers with a few\nembedded storms to push through south-central into northeast \nNebraska, perhaps all the way down to I-80. This will clear by\nmid-morning but with strong isentropic upglide across the \nregion through the day, I'd expect to continue to see areas of \nlight rain continuing into the afternoon. The next round of\nheavier rain will arrive tomorrow evening, this time more across\nsoutheast Nebraska into southwest Iowa. A swath of 1-2 inches of\nrain is expected with this round, looking more likely around\nBeatrice to just north of Nebraska City. \n\nAdditional shortwaves will continue to bring periods of heavier\nrainfall into Wednesday with heaviest rainfall expected\novernight Tuesday night and again Wednesday night. We're\nwatching Wednesday night for some potential for severe storms.\nWednesday night we see the remnant surface low from Hurricane\nPolo lift north across central Kansas into southeast Nebraska.\nThis will steepen lapse rates, increasing environmental\ninstability. We'll also see fairly good low-level shear along\nthe warm front lifting north. Right now the greatest severe\npotential is to our south with the surface warm front not quite\nmaking it into southeast Nebraska, but if it does push farther \nnorth, we could see potential for wind and tornadoes shift north\ninto our area.\n\nWednesday evening may be the time we see heaviest rainfall \nnortheast of the surface low. At this time we see strong \nmoisture advection overtop of the surface frontal boundary. PWAT\nvalues around 2.1 to 2.2 inches is well in excess of our\nclimatological max of 1.72 inches for October 1st, or even most\nof the month of September. Every deterministic model I bring up\nshows 2.1 to 2.2 inches across much of eastern Nebraska into\nwestern Iowa. This will create highly efficient rainfall with\nensemble guidance highlighting potential for a broad area of\n1-2 inches of rain over a 6-hr period. This on top of already\nsaturated ground really increases the potential for river\nflooding Wednesday night going into Thursday with lingering\nriver impacts potentially into Friday-Saturday on main-stem\nrivers such as the Platte and Missouri (south of the Platte). \n\nRainfall clears off to the southeast Thursday as the upper-level\ntrough shears away with the cold front sinking south of our\narea. Skies may clear Thursday night into early Friday\npotentially leading to some significant widespread fog Friday\nmorning. Going into the weekend, we're going to see a period of\nquiet weather with a decaying ridge building over the region.\nWe'll be glanced by the southern edge of a Canadian High keeping\ntemperatures on the cooler side Friday and Saturday. Saturday\nnight a dry cold front looks to move through bringing a\nreinforcing surge of cooler air for early next week keeping\nhighs in the low-to-mid 60s and lows in the upper 40s to low\n50s.",
